---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Sessions 16&ndash;19: Build (Iteration 1)</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Racquet Ball Launcher Project &mdash; Milestone 2 of 6</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <h2>This Is a Work Period, Not a Lesson</h2>
          <p>
            There's no new concept to introduce today &mdash; you already
            have everything you need from your Design Packet. These four
            sessions are for turning your concept sketch into a physical
            launcher.
          </p>

          <p>
            Teams will move at different speeds, and that's expected. A
            mechanism that looked simple on paper often needs a mid-build
            redesign once you're actually holding the materials &mdash;
            that's normal engineering, not a sign you did Sessions 14&ndash;15
            wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Bungee safety still applies every
            session</b>, not just the day you got certified. Recheck anchor
            points and cord condition every time you test a pull.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Construct Your Mechanism</h3>
          <p>
            Work from your Design Packet's concept sketch. If reality forces
            a change from what you sketched, that's fine &mdash; note the
            change and why in your notebook as you go, not after the fact.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Troubleshoot As You Go</h3>
          <p>
            Expect real problems: parts that don't fit, a mechanism that
            binds, an attachment point that isn't strong enough. Diagnose
            and adjust &mdash; this is Build and Iterate happening together,
            the same way Session 1's design cycle described.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Checkpoint Timing</h3>
          <p>
            At the Session 18&ndash;19 spot-check, your instructor checks
            every team: is your mechanism physically functional, regardless
            of polish? A team that's badly behind at that point needs to flag
            it now, not wait until the milestone ends.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>If Your Team Finishes Early</h3>
          <p>
            Don't sit idle, and don't start "polishing" indefinitely.
            Start preliminary data collection &mdash; a few practice pulls,
            logged the same way you will for real in Sessions 20&ndash;22.
            It's a genuine head start, not busywork.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>By the Session 18&ndash;19 spot-check, your instructor confirms:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Your mechanism physically exists and can complete a launch cycle</li>
            <li>
              Your team can point to your chosen IV's physical control point
              on the mechanism (where pull distance is measured, or where
              angle is set)
            </li>
            <li>No bungee safety protocol from Sessions 14&ndash;15 is being skipped</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            "Functional" is the bar here, not "finished" or "polished" &mdash;
            that distinction matters, because Sessions 20&ndash;22 need real
            time, not a squeezed leftover.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Which of your decision matrix's four categories &mdash; Cost, Feasibility, Time, Alignment &mdash; turned out to matter most once you were actually building? Did your matrix predict that?</li>
            <li>Sessions 20&ndash;22 ask you to test across a range of settings for your IV. Looking at your finished mechanism, what do you expect the realistic range of settings to be?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            No written deliverable this milestone &mdash; this is graded as
            a hard checkpoint via your instructor's direct spot-check, plus
            your individual reflection.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Milestone Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
